Reason: The original `xe_vm_madvise_ioctl()` only validated PAT indices for SVM/userptr vmas with L2 flush optimization. It lacked any check for the combination of `XE_COH_NONE` coherency mode and CPU cached memory (e.g., WB caching or imported dma-buf without caching info). This allowed userspace to set non-coherent access on CPU cached buffers, which on iGPU enables GPU reads to bypass CPU caches and read stale sensitive data from DRAM.

---

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
static bool check_pat_args_are_sane(struct xe_device *xe,
                                    struct xe_vmas_in_madvise_range *madvise_range,
                                    u16 pat_index)
{
    u16 coh_mode = xe_pat_index_get_coh_mode(xe, pat_index);
    int i;
    if (coh_mode != XE_COH_NONE || IS_DGFX(xe))
        return true;
    for (i = 0; i < madvise_range->num_vmas; i++) {
        struct xe_vma *vma = madvise_range->vmas[i];
        struct xe_bo *bo = xe_vma_bo(vma);
        if (bo) {
            if (XE_IOCTL_DBG(xe, bo->cpu_caching == DRM_XE_GEM_CPU_CACHING_WB))
                return false;
            if (XE_IOCTL_DBG(xe, !bo->cpu_caching))
                return false;
        } else if (XE_IOCTL_DBG(xe, xe_vma_is_cpu_addr_mirror(vma) ||
                                    xe_vma_is_userptr(vma)))
            return false;
    }
    return true;
}
```
```c
if (args->type == DRM_XE_MEM_RANGE_ATTR_PAT) {
    if (!check_pat_args_are_sane(xe, &madvise_range,
                                 args->pat_index.val)) {
        err = -EINVAL;
        goto free_vmas;
    }
}
```
Reason: The new function `check_pat_args_are_sane()` rejects PAT indices with `XE_COH_NONE` coherency when any VMA in the range is backed by CPU cached memory (WB buffers, imported dma-buf without caching info, userptr/SVM). It skips this check for dGPU because such platforms lack a non‑coherent system memory access path that would violate DMA coherency. The call in `xe_vm_madvise_ioctl()` ensures the validation is performed before applying the madvise attribute, preventing the security issue.

